{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788264325,"nonce":"bn6gd8TDR3fSa6pxYa8xe/k5YG8=","title":"Combat posture is a standing state, not three verbs remembered in the right order: a posture verb, and posture in the digest","message":"Lesson `[[seek-off-is-a-decision-to-flee]]` rescoped as code, per Evan's ruling of 2026-09-01 that **a deterministic finding goes in the mod, not in a note** (DESIGN decisions log; `postmortem.md`'s mod rung, now MANDATORY).\n\n## The evidence this exists to answer\n\nM1 run `m1-20260831`, the first link in the chain that killed two colonists.\n\nDay 1: **seek-at-will ON as a standing posture marched all three unarmed colonists 60+ cells at a fogged insect hive.** It was turned OFF to stop that — and turning it off silently put them back on the vanilla flee branch, which is the branch they took when the manhunter crow arrived. Captain fled **150 cells into unexplored ground**, went down there, and died before the last colonist could cross the distance.\n\nBoth halves were correct decisions in isolation. The failure is that \"seek off\" is not a neutral state — it is a decision to flee — and nothing in the surface said so at the moment it mattered.\n\nRelated, and verified rather than assumed: `seek-at-will`'s `before`/`after` blocks report `hostility_response: \"Flee\"` even when seek is ON. That echo is TRUTHFUL — SeekAndKill's `ThinkTreeInjector` inserts `ThinkNode_ConditionalSeekAndKill -\u003e JobGiver_SquadSeek` above `ThinkNode_ConditionalColonist`, so with seek ON the vanilla flee node is unreachable and the field simply describes a node nothing consults. Truthful about the field, misleading about behaviour. (Op 109 of the run has the pair: seek ON, hostility `Flee`, Captain in `JobDriver_FleeAndCower`.) A posture read fixes this by reporting what will HAPPEN rather than what a field says.\n\n## Why this qualifies for the mod rung\n\nPosture is three settings that must agree — allowed area, seek, hostility response — and whether they agree is computable from state already published. There is no judgement in checking the combination; the judgement is only in choosing which posture to hold, which stays in the playbook.\n\n## Scope\n\n**`SeekVerbs.cs` — a composite standing-posture verb.** Binds every violence-capable pawn to an `Area_Allowed`, sets seek ON where eligible, and sets `hostility: \"Attack\"` as the backstop. Reports per-pawn refusals — a pawn who cannot take the posture (incapable of violence, downed, drafted) is a real answer, and the verb must name each rather than partially succeeding in silence. This is a player-facing verb: re-implement its precondition and cite the widget member, per DESIGN §Action model.\n\n**`DigestVerb.cs` — publish `posture`.** `{will_seek: n/m, area_bound: n/m}`, so the standing state is visible at every read instead of inferred from a field that describes an unreachable node. Consider also publishing what the pawns will actually DO on contact, since that is the question the `hostility_response` echo appears to answer and does not.\n\nThe three settings the M1 post-run fix list names — an `Area_Allowed` bounding where colonists work, seek ON as a standing posture, and `assign {hostility:\"Attack\"}` — are exactly this verb's body. They were written down as three things to remember.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- One verb sets all three and reports per-pawn what it did and what it refused.\n- A pawn incapable of violence is refused by name, not skipped silently.\n- `digest` publishes `posture` with `will_seek` and `area_bound` as n/m.\n- The posture survives a save/load round trip, or the digest says it did not.\n- `checklists/` colony-start item 12 shrinks to \"confirm the posture verb ran\".\n- `[[seek-off-is-a-decision-to-flee]]` keeps only the WHY and the policy choice.","files":null}]}